# App-declared runtime variables — copy to `.app.env` to run the local workbench on the
# same seam the deployed environments use.
#
#   cp .app.env.example .app.env
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY FROM .env
#
# Two seams reach the backend containers, and they are not interchangeable:
#
#   .app.env   the variables this app DECLARES in environment.schema.json. Studio
#              renders exactly the declared keys into this file, per environment.
#              The compose profiles forward it (`env_file:`). This is the seam that
#              carries app configuration to production.
#
#   .env       compose's own variable file, read automatically from this directory.
#              It feeds the `${...}` interpolations in docker-compose.yml — the
#              platform's wiring (SECRET_KEY, API_PORT, WEB_PORT) and values only the
#              host can resolve. Gitignored; yours alone.
#
# Compose resolves `environment:` OVER `env_file:`. So if a name appears in both a
# compose `environment:` block and this file, the compose value wins and this file's
# copy is silently discarded. `terp verify --only env-seams` fails on that overlap
# rather than letting it be discovered in a deployed environment.
#
# `.app.env` is gitignored: it holds environment-specific values, and may hold secrets.
# Do not commit it, and do not hand-edit it in an environment Studio manages — there,
# Studio owns and rewrites it.
#
# HOST vs CONTAINER vs BROWSER
#
# An address is resolved by exactly one of three parties, and one value cannot be right
# for two of them. Declare which in environment.schema.json ("resolvedBy"), because it
# is not derivable from a variable's name or type:
#
#   container   dialled by a service on the compose network -> use the SERVICE NAME
#               (http://api:8000). A loopback address here is the container itself.
#   host        dialled from your shell, outside the network -> http://127.0.0.1:8000
#   browser     the user's browser is sent here -> a host address (http://localhost:5173)
#
# Declared variables and their defaults: `terp inspect` the manifest, or read
# environment.schema.json. Full explanation: `terp guide environment`.

# This app declares no runtime variables yet. When it does, add them here with values
# suitable for the local workbench — e.g.:
#
# EXAMPLE_API_BASE_URL=http://api:8000
